2. Problem Statement and Formalized Threat Model

2.1 Root Cause

The root cause of prompt injection is the collapse of instruction and data channels into a single token stream. In standard autoregressive transformer architectures, the model cannot differentiate between a system prompt, a user query, a retrieved document, or a multimodal sensory input. Any external content that enters the context window can act as an instruction, violating the integrity of the agent’s goal. This is not a model weakness but an architectural design flaw: untrusted data is treated as trusted instruction through system design.

2.2 Formalized Threat Model

We adopt a strong, layered adversary model that explicitly includes adaptive and multimodal capabilities, drawing on recent formalizations [9, 10, 11] and the OWASP Top‑10 for Agentic Applications [12] and the Cloud Security Alliance Agentic Trust Framework (ATF) threat taxonomy [13].

Attacker Classes:

· 𝒜ₛ (Static Attacker): Controls arbitrary external data sources (web pages, API responses, PDFs, emails) and injects adversarial payloads crafted offline without knowledge of the defense internals. This corresponds to the traditional “context‑insensitive” model.
· 𝒜꜀ (Context‑Adaptive Attacker): Observes partial agent state (current task, tool outputs, intermediate reasoning) before crafting and injecting payloads. This attacker adapts attacks to the agent’s dynamic context, as formalized in ARGUS [10].
· 𝒜ᵥᵥ (Adaptive White‑Box Attacker): Possesses full knowledge of the defense architecture and iteratively probes the system to craft targeted bypasses. This class subsumes 𝒜꜀ and represents the strongest realistic threat, as demonstrated in [7].
· 𝒜ₘ (Multimodal Attacker): Injects adversarial payloads through non‑text modalities—images, audio, sensor data—that are processed by multimodal agents. Visual perturbations achieve 60–80% attack success rates on vision‑language models [14], and physical‑world attacks (e.g., malicious road signs) can hijack embodied AI agents [15].

In multi‑agent settings, any attacker class may control one or more agents, which can arbitrarily deviate from their specified protocol. The attacker does not directly access agent code, system prompts, or cryptographic keys.

Attack Vectors (Mapped to OWASP ASI categories [12]):

· Direct injection (ASI01): User‑supplied input containing instruction‑like text.
· Indirect injection via tools (ASI01, ASI02): Retrieved documents, API responses, or code execution output carrying hidden instructions.
· Cross‑agent injection (ASI07): Malicious agent outputs relayed through trusted communication channels.
· Memory poisoning (ASI06): Long‑term memory entries crafted to mislead future reasoning.
· Multimodal injection (ASI01, ASI02): Adversarial instructions embedded in images, audio, or sensor streams.

Security Properties to Preserve [9, 16]:

· Goal integrity: Executed actions align with the user’s original intent.
· Instruction provenance: No instruction originates from an untrusted source without explicit, verified authorization.
· Non‑impersonation: The identity and authority of every agent are cryptographically verifiable.
· Memory consistency: Persistent knowledge is free of contradictions and verifiable against a trusted knowledge base.
· Capability preservation: No defense mechanism shall reduce benign task completion below an operationally defined threshold (the Autonomy Tax constraint [6]).
· Auditability: All agent actions are traceable to an accountable identity and reconstructable post‑hoc for forensic and compliance purposes [17].

