7. Agent Identity Lifecycle Management

Enterprise‑scale deployments face an NHI (Non‑Human Identity) explosion: 53% of organizations now manage more non‑human identities than human employees, with 23% reporting a ratio of six‑to‑one or higher [35]. Only 12% have comprehensive automated lifecycle management for machine identities [36]. International estimates project non‑human and agentic identities could exceed 45 billion by end‑2026 [37].

Our Signed Communication Protocol (Layer 2) must address this scale. We propose lifecycle phases aligned with emerging practices [38]:

· Provisioning: Every agent receives a DID at instantiation with scoped capabilities and an expiration timestamp.
· Active management: Continuous monitoring of agent behavior against granted permissions; automated credential rotation.
· Decommissioning: Automatic revocation of all credentials and audit trail finalization upon agent termination.
· Kill‑switch integration: Aligned with the CSA ATF Incident Response element, a centralized kill switch must be capable of revoking all credentials for a compromised agent across all systems within minutes. Currently, 55% of organizations lack this capability [35].

