Appendix B: Extended Threat Model — Attacker Class Formalization

---

B.1 Attacker Hierarchy

The four attacker classes form a strict capability hierarchy:
\mathcal{A}_S \subsetneq \mathcal{A}_C \subsetneq \mathcal{A}_W \quad (\text{text‑domain}),


\mathcal{A}_M \not\subset \mathcal{A}_W,\; \mathcal{A}_W \not\subset \mathcal{A}_M \quad (\text{orthogonal surfaces}).


$\mathcal{A}_M$ is not subsumed by $\mathcal{A}_W$ because the attack surface (vision/audio/sensor encoders) is architecturally distinct.

B.2 Static Attacker — $\mathcal{A}_S$

Capabilities: Controls external data (web pages, APIs, PDFs). Payloads crafted offline without defense knowledge. Complexity: $O(1)$—one crafted document suffices.

Coverage: Fully addressed by Layer 1 (ASIDE/PCFI), Layer 3 (TOF schema enforcement), Layer 5 (provenance tagging). Weakest class.

B.3 Context‑Adaptive Attacker — $\mathcal{A}_C$

Capabilities: Observes partial agent state (current task, tool outputs, intermediate reasoning) via a state oracle $\mathcal{O}(s_i) \to \hat{s}_i$. Payloads are functions of this state: $p = \phi(\hat{s}_i)$. Attack success depends on context.

Why static defenses fail: PCFI and ASIDE operate at input level, before agent state exists; they cannot distinguish a context‑plausible injection from a legitimate workflow continuation.

Coverage: Partial. ARGUS [10] reduces ASR to 3.8% against adaptive white‑box adversaries by tracking provenance; it is the primary defense. Residual risk remains for payloads that exploit narrow legitimate task semantics.

B.4 Adaptive White‑Box Attacker — $\mathcal{A}_W$

Capabilities: Full knowledge of defense architecture (ASIDE’s $R$, PCFI rules, A‑MemGuard threshold $\tau$, AgentArmor’s type graph, ARGUS provenance logic). Iteratively probes the system: payload $p_r = \phi_r(D, \{p_1,\dots,p_{r-1}\}, \{b_1,\dots,b_{r-1}\})$ where $b_r$ is defense outcome, until bypass.

Known vulnerabilities: NAACL 2025 study [7] showed >50 % ASR for twelve published defenses under adaptive attack. No current mechanism offers formal adversarial robustness proofs. Cryptographic layers (L2, partially L4) resist through computational hardness; content‑based layers (L1, L3, L5, L6) lack formal guarantees.

Coverage: Probabilistic only. ARGUS claims robustness but requires third‑party validation.

B.5 Multimodal Attacker — $\mathcal{A}_M$

Capabilities: Injects via vision, audio, or sensor channels processed by multimodal/embodied agents. Visual injection achieves 60–80 % ASR [14]; physical‑world attacks (CHAI, [15]) require no digital access.

Framework gap: Layers 1, 3, 6 operate on text tokens only; they provide zero coverage for inputs passing through vision encoders $f_V$, audio encoders $f_A$, or sensor encoders $f_S$. For any modality $m \in \{V,A,S\}$, a payload $p_m$ is invisible to all seven layers as specified.

Mitigation path: Glass Wall [19] addresses multimodal visual injections in its benchmark. Extending ASIDE‑style orthogonal rotation to vision‑encoder output tokens is a conjectured but unverified extension.

