Appendix E: Agent Identity Lifecycle — Formal Specification

---

E.1 Lifecycle State Machine

States:
\text{UNREGISTERED} \xrightarrow{\text{Provision}} \text{ACTIVE} \xrightarrow{\text{Suspend}} \text{SUSPENDED} \xrightarrow{\text{Reinstate}} \text{ACTIVE}


\text{ACTIVE} \xrightarrow{\text{Revoke}} \text{REVOKED}


\text{SUSPENDED} \xrightarrow{\text{Revoke}} \text{REVOKED}


\text{ACTIVE} \xrightarrow{\text{Expire}} \text{EXPIRED}

Provision $(A, \text{scope}, T_{\text{exp}})$: Creates identity $\text{id}(A)$ with DID, generates Ed25519 key pair, assigns capability scope $S \subseteq \mathcal{T} \cup \mathcal{R}$, sets expiration $T_{\text{exp}}$. Precondition: provisioning authority holds ATTP L3+ credential. Post‑condition: $\text{id}(A)$ broadcast to federated registry.

Revoke $(A, \text{reason})$: Broadcasts signed revocation to all verifiers. Revocation must propagate within $\Delta T_{\text{revoke}} \le 60$ s (kill‑switch requirement). Bindu’s distributed ledger provides eventual consistency; the 60 s bound requires consensus finality compatible with the deployment’s threat model.

Rotate $(A, pk_{\text{new}})$: Replaces signing key while preserving identity continuity. Rotation on schedule ($T_{\text{max}} \le 90$ d standard, $\le 30$ d high‑assurance), on compromise indication, and on container restart.

E.2 Capability Delegation Protocol

Delegation is represented as a Verifiable Credential (VC):
\text{VC}_{A_1 \to A_2} = \left\{ \text{issuer}: \text{id}(A_1),\ \text{subject}: \text{id}(A_2),\ \text{scope}: S' \subseteq S_{A_1},\ \text{expiry}: T_{\text{exp}}' \le T_{\text{exp}}^{A_1},\ \text{sig}: \text{Sign}(sk_{A_1}, \cdot) \right\}

Anti‑privilege‑escalation constraints:

1. $S' \subseteq S_{A_1}$ (cannot delegate unheld capabilities).
2. $T_{\text{exp}}' \le T_{\text{exp}}^{A_1}$ (delegation not exceed credential lifetime).
3. Delegation depth $d \le d_{\text{max}}$ (recommended $d_{\text{max}}=3$) enforced at policy engine.
4. Non‑transitive by default; re‑delegation requires delegatable: true and L4 trust level.

E.3 NHI Scale — Architectural Requirements

At enterprise scale, the system must support:

· Registration throughput $\ge 10^4$ agent provisioning events/s.
· Revocation propagation $\le 60$ s full‑fleet.
· Identity verification latency $\le 5$ ms (L1), $\le 50$ ms (L3 full capability check).
· Storage: ~2 KB per identity. At $10^9$ identities, registry exceeds 2 TB; federated sharding (Bindu) required.

