Appendix F: Post‑Quantum Cryptography Migration Roadmap

---

F.1 Threat Timeline

Current ATTP and Bindu rely on ECDSA/Ed25519, broken by Shor’s algorithm on a cryptographically relevant quantum computer (CRQC). “Harvest now, decrypt later” threatens long‑lived sensitive agent communications. NIST published ML‑DSA (CRYSTALS‑Dilithium, FIPS 204), ML‑KEM (CRYSTALS‑Kyber, FIPS 203), and SLH‑DSA (SPHINCS+, FIPS 205) in August 2024; the federal migration target is 2030.

F.2 Migration Phases

Phase 1 (Immediate): Hybrid Signatures. Double‑bag each message with ECDSA + ML‑DSA (Dilithium3). Both signatures must verify. Provides classical and quantum security today, with ~51× increase in signature size (3,293 B vs. 64 B).

Phase 2 (2027 target): Native ML‑DSA. All agent identities transition to ML‑DSA signing. DID documents updated to advertise PQC key types via Multikey entries with algorithm identifier ML‑DSA-65.

Phase 3 (2030 target): Full PQC Infrastructure. Extend ML‑KEM to agent‑to‑agent encrypted channels; implement crypto‑agility via sidecar proxy pattern.

F.3 Implementation Constraint

The 51× signature payload increase under Dilithium3 adds latency. For throughput‑sensitive deployments, SLH‑DSA‑SHAKE‑128s offers 49 B signatures at ~10× slower signing. Algorithm choice is deployment‑specific; crypto‑agility ensures configurable without architectural change.

